  let go. The layers of fascia then peel apart along winding lines, often with the body turning and twisting; places are
  left hollow, the hardest part, at times with nausea; and over days to weeks they fill in again, felt as re-stitching,
  which brings back a sense of wholeness. In any one region the knots go first and the layers after, while across the body
  the stages interleave. The whole course can run for months or years, and its last filling-in can be slow, uncomfortable
  rather than painful. People do come through it; those who have say it is lived through rather than stopped. The reports are many but informal; the process is little known, and we know of no study of
  it as such. It belongs to a wider
  record of bodily experience in intensive practice, which includes pressure and tension that build and release, currents of
  energy and involuntary movement ${c('lindahl2017')}, and energy-like somatic experiences whose course, effects and
